.TH SH-CONTROL 1
.SH NAME
control \- shell script interface to control(2)'s transfer functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load control

.B id := ${control new}
.br
.B control
.I id
.BR num | den
.I coefficient...
.br
.B control
.I id
.B pid
.I kp ki kd taud
.br
.B control
.I id
.BR series | parallel
.I id2
.br
.B control
.I id
.B feedback
.I id2
.RI [ sign ]
.br
.B control
.I id
.BR step | impulse
.I dt n
.br
.B control
.I id
.B bode
.I wlo whi n
.br
.B control
.I id
.B clear
.br
.B ${control
.I id
.BR num | den | text | order | dcgain | poles | zeros | stable | routh
.B }
.br
.B ${control
.I id
.BR trace | names | rows | columns | margins | metrics
.B }
.br
.B control del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Control
makes
.IR control (2)
usable from
.IR sh (1).
.PP
Coefficients go in
.B highest
.B power
.BR first ,
which is
.IR control (2)'s
order and the order a polynomial is written down in, so
.B control
.I id
.B den 1 4 16
is
.IR s \u\s-22\s+2\d+4\fIs\fP+16.
The transfer function is built the first time something needs it, so the
numerator and denominator may be set in either order and there is no
.B commit
to remember.
.PP
.BR Series ,
.B parallel
and
.B feedback
combine this handle with another and replace this one's transfer
function with the result;
.B feedback
defaults to \-1, the negative feedback a control loop is made of.
.PP
In the
.B ${...}
form,
.B num
and
.B den
return the coefficients instead of setting them, so one handle's
transfer function can be copied into another:
.IP
.EX
control $l num ${control $c num}
control $l den ${control $c den}
control $l series $p
.EE
.PP
That is what combining systems in a script needs, since
.B series
and
.B feedback
replace a handle's transfer function in place.
.SS Responses
.BR Step ,
.B impulse
and
.B bode
each record a response, which
.B trace
then returns as one flat list \- the shape
.IR sh-plot (1)'s
.B append
takes.
.B Names
gives the columns:
.B t y
for a response,
.B w mag phase
for a sweep.
.PP
.B Bode
spaces its frequencies evenly in the
.IR logarithm ,
because that is how the plot is read and a linear sweep wastes nearly
all its points at the top of the range.
.SS Answers
.B Poles
and
.B zeros
return real and imaginary parts in pairs.
.B Stable
is 1 or 0;
.B routh
is the number of roots in the right half plane, arrived at without
finding any of them.
.PP
.B Margins
returns the gain margin, the frequency it occurs at, the phase margin
.B in
.B degrees
and its frequency.
A margin that does not exist comes back as the word
.B none
rather than a large number, because no gain margin at all is not the
same as a big one \- an integrator's phase never reaches \-180 degrees.
.PP
.B Metrics
describes the last step response:
.BR settled ,
then the final value, the 10-to-90 rise time, the peak, the time of the
peak, the overshoot as a fraction, and the settling time.
.PP
.B Settled
comes first because it says whether to believe the rest.
Everything else is computed from the final value, and the final value is
the average of the last tenth of the record \- so a run that stops
before the response finishes reports a final value that is merely where
the curve happened to be, and an overshoot measured against it.
That is not a small error: a loop sampled for two thirds of one
oscillation reported an overshoot of 0.037 where the answer was 0.139.
When
.B settled
is 0, lengthen the run.
.SH EXAMPLE
A second-order system, and the loop every course opens with:
.IP
.EX
load std
load control
g := ${control new}
control $g num 16
control $g den 1 4 16
control $g step 0.005 1000
echo ${control $g metrics}	# 1 (settled), then 0.1629 overshoot

p := ${control new}
control $p num 1
control $p den 1 3 2 0
echo ${control $p margins}	# 6 at 1.4142, 53.4 degrees
control del $g $p
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/control.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR sh-circuit (1),
.IR control (2),
.IR controltest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A numerator of higher degree than the denominator, a zero denominator,
an impulse response that would contain a delta, a derivative term with
no filter: each fails the command with the message
.IR control (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Control (2)
underneath it is checked by
.IR controltest (1),
and what is added here \- handle bookkeeping, the logarithmic sweep and
the flattening of the trace \- is exercised only by the example above
and by
.BR /lib/demo/pidlab.sh .
.PP
.B Metrics
refuses to describe anything but a step response, so a caller who wants
the rise time of an impulse response, or of a response computed some
other way, cannot have it.
.PP
There is no root locus, for the same reason
.IR control (2)
has none: it is a sweep of the roots over gain, which the pieces here
support, and the plotting is the substance of it.
